        "topic": "TypeScript",
        "question": "What is TypeScript, and how is it different from JavaScript?",
        "answer": "TypeScript is a superset of JavaScript that adds static typing, interfaces, and modern features to improve code maintainability and catch errors at compile time. Unlike JavaScript, which is dynamically typed, TypeScript enforces type safety.",
        "keyPoints": [
            "TypeScript provides optional static typing.",
            "Allows developers to define interfaces and custom types.",
            "Compiles down to plain JavaScript.",
            "Supports modern JavaScript features with better tooling."
        ],
        "codeExample": "let message: string = 'Hello, TypeScript!';",
